# Howdy Auth

`howdy_auth` is an optional package that signs people in to a Howdy app. It
gives you:

- sign-in by a token sent to the visitor's email address, with no password
  to keep
- optional [passwords](/docs/auth/passwords) alongside the email tokens
- [social sign-in](/docs/auth/providers) with Google, Apple, GitHub,
  Facebook and Microsoft Entra
- [passkeys](/docs/auth/passkeys) and
  [multi-factor authentication](/docs/auth/mfa)
- [enterprise single sign-on](/docs/auth/sso) over OpenID Connect and SAML
- a [guard](/docs/controllers/guards) that hands your handlers the signed-in
  user
- JSON endpoints, minimal starter pages, and the same operations as plain
  functions for when you bring your own pages
- [groups](/docs/auth/groups) for workspaces and tenants, and
  [fields](/docs/auth/fields) for small facts about a user
- separate [roles and permissions](/docs/auth/roles)

It stores everything in your app's own database, through the `gloo` Repo you
already have, using [Howdy Database](/docs/database/overview). SQLite and
PostgreSQL both work. Your app owns the connection and supplies the function
that sends email; the package never opens a database or talks to a mail
server itself.

An account is identified by its *email address*. There is no username login
built in, though a username fits well as a field: see
[Username and password sign-in](/docs/guides/username-and-password). SCIM,
invitations by email and a hosted management dashboard are not
implemented.

## Install

Add the packages to `gleam.toml`, next to `howdy`:

```toml
howdy_auth = { git = "https://github.com/mikeyjones/howdy.git", ref = "v2.1.1", path = "auth" }
howdy_database = { git = "https://github.com/mikeyjones/howdy.git", ref = "v2.1.1", path = "database" }
```

`howdy_database` provides the `howdy/migration` module you run the auth
migrations with. Then download them:

```sh
gleam deps download
```

Nothing native is built: passwords are hashed with OTP's own `crypto`.

## Create the tables

The package brings its own [migrations](/docs/auth/migrations). Run them
before the app starts, with the same Repo your app uses:

```gleam
import howdy/auth
import howdy/authorization
import howdy/migration

pub fn migrate(db) {
  migration.run(db, [auth.schema(), authorization.schema()])
}
```

Leave out `authorization.schema()` if you do not need roles. The tables are
named `howdy_auth_*` and `howdy_authz_*`, and the package checks at startup
that nobody has changed them by hand.

## Configure

Build one `Auth` value when the app starts, and share it:

```gleam
import howdy/auth

pub fn identity(db, send_email) {
  let assert Ok(identity) =
    auth.new(
      repo: db,
      origin: "https://app.example.com",
      deliver: send_email,
    )
  auth.allow_registration(identity)
}
```

- `repo` is your database. With SQLite, auth and your own code can share
  it, as long as your code goes through
  [`howdy/database`](/docs/database/transactions). If your code calls
  `gloo/repo` directly while requests are being served, give auth a second
  Repo of its own, opened on the same file.
- `origin` is the public address of your site: scheme, host and optional
  port. It must be `https`, except for `http://localhost`,
  `http://127.0.0.1` and `http://[::1]` while you develop. Auth never trusts
  the `host` header to work this out.
- `deliver` sends an email. See [Sending the email](/docs/auth/signing-in).
  An app that only uses providers can use `auth.new_without_email` instead.

Everything else is opt-in, and returns a new `Auth`:

- `auth.allow_registration(identity)` lets visitors create accounts.
  Without it, only [provisioned](/docs/auth/administration) users exist.
- `auth.with_passwords(identity)` turns on [passwords](/docs/auth/passwords).
- `auth.with_provider(identity, provider)` adds a
  [social sign-in provider](/docs/auth/providers).
- `auth.with_passkeys(identity, name)` turns on
  [passkeys](/docs/auth/passkeys).
- `auth.with_mfa(identity, config)` turns on
  [second factors](/docs/auth/mfa).
- `auth.with_sso(identity, config)` turns on
  [single sign-on connections](/docs/auth/sso).
- `auth.with_policy(identity, policy)` changes
  [lifetimes and limits](/docs/auth/sessions).
- `auth.with_multi_session(identity, max:)` lets one browser stay signed in
  to [several accounts](/docs/auth/sessions).
- `auth.with_groups(identity, mode)` turns on [groups](/docs/auth/groups).
- `auth.with_session_store(identity, store)` keeps
  [sessions](/docs/auth/sessions) somewhere other than the database.
- `auth.with_account_deletion` and `auth.with_email_change_approval` shape
  [account changes](/docs/auth/account).

## Mount the routes

```gleam
import howdy
import howdy/auth/pages
import howdy/auth/routes

pub fn app(identity) {
  howdy.new()
  |> howdy.controller(routes.api(identity, at: "/api/auth"))
  |> howdy.controller(pages.routes(identity, at: "/auth", api_at: "/api/auth"))
}
```

`routes.api` is the JSON API. `pages.routes` adds starter pages that call
it: `/auth/register`, `/auth/login`, `/auth/mfa` and `/auth/account`, plus
`/auth/password/register` and `/auth/password/login` when passwords are on.
The account page grows controls for whatever you turn on: passwords,
passkeys, second factors, linked providers, sessions and account changes.
The pages are deliberately plain and need JavaScript. Leave `pages.routes`
out to write your own; [Signing in](/docs/auth/signing-in) lists the
endpoints they would call.

[Providers](/docs/auth/providers) and [SSO](/docs/auth/sso) sign in through
browser redirects, and have routes of their own: `routes.providers` and
`routes.sso`.

## Protect your routes

`auth.required(identity)` is a [guard](/docs/controllers/guards). On a
guarded controller, the signed-in user arrives on `ctx.guard`:

```gleam
import howdy/auth
import howdy/auth/user
import howdy/controller

pub fn account_controller(identity) {
  controller.guarded("/account", auth.required(identity))
  |> controller.get("/me", fn(ctx) {
    controller.json(ctx, user.to_json(ctx.guard.user))
  })
  |> controller.build()
}
```

For one route on an ordinary controller, use `guard.require`:

```gleam
use principal <- guard.require(ctx, auth.required(identity))
```

The value is a `user.Principal`: the `user`, a `session_id`, and the
`client` the request came from. The user is a `user.User` with an `id`, an
`email`, a `group_id`, and `created_at` and `updated_at` timestamps.

The guard accepts a session cookie or an `Authorization: Bearer` header,
and rejects a request that carries both. A visitor who is not signed in gets
`401` with `{"error":"unauthorized"}`; the guard never redirects to a login
page, so that choice stays with your app. A visitor who has passed their
first factor but not yet their [second](/docs/auth/mfa) is not signed in.

A write made with the cookie must carry an `origin` header matching the
configured origin, which is what browsers send. That protects your own
guarded routes from [CSRF](/docs/middleware/csrf) as well as the package's.
Do not change anything in a `GET` handler.

## Testing

Auth works with [`howdy/testing`](/docs/guides/testing). Give each test an
in-memory database, and a `deliver` function that hands the token to the
test instead of sending it:

```gleam
import gleam/erlang/process
import gloo/adapter/sqlite
import howdy/auth/secret

pub fn register_test() {
  let assert Ok(db) = sqlite.start(sqlite.memory())
  let assert Ok(_) = my_app.migrate(db)
  let inbox = process.new_subject()
  let identity =
    my_app.identity(db, fn(delivery: auth.Delivery) {
      process.send(inbox, secret.reveal(delivery.token))
      Ok(Nil)
    })

  let res =
    testing.post(
      "/api/auth/register",
      json.object([#("email", json.string("ada@example.com"))]),
    )
    |> testing.send(my_app.app(identity))
  assert res.status == 202

  let assert Ok(token) = process.receive(inbox, 1000)
  let assert Ok(session) = auth.exchange(identity, token)
  assert session.user.email == "ada@example.com"
}
```
